          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 42-year-old woman presents with a solitary thyroid nodule. Her serum TSH is fo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 42-year-old woman presents with a solitary thyroid nodule. Her serum TSH is found to be suppressed. What is the most appropriate next investigation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fine-needle aspiration cytology</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>FNAC is appropriate when TSH is normal or raised. A suppressed TSH suggests an autonomously functioning nodule, which should first be confirmed with a radionuclide scan before deciding on aspiration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Radionuclide thyroid scan</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A suppressed TSH indicates a possibly autonomous ('hot') nodule. A radionuclide scan confirms autonomous function, and hot nodules are rarely malignant.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Contrast-enhanced CT of the neck</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CT is reserved for staging confirmed malignancy and assessing retrosternal extension — not for the initial work-up of a nodule with suppressed TSH.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Core needle biopsy of the nodule</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Core needle biopsy is not the standard initial investigation for thyroid nodules. FNAC is the tissue-sampling method of choice, and even that follows a radionuclide scan when TSH is suppressed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: suppressed TSH → radionuclide scan first. Normal or raised TSH → ultrasound → FNAC. This sequence prevents unnecessary biopsies of benign hot nodules."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The TSH-first rule is key: a suppressed TSH redirects the pathway to a radionuclide scan, because hot nodules are rarely malignant and are not routinely aspirated."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
