          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with medullary carcinoma of the thyroid is told that radioactive iodin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with medullary carcinoma of the thyroid is told that radioactive iodine therapy will not be effective for this tumour. What is the reason?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Medullary carcinoma metastasises too rapidly for radioiodine to act</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The limitation is not about metastatic speed. Radioiodine works by being taken up intracellularly through iodide transporters. The cell of origin determines whether uptake occurs.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It arises from parafollicular C cells, which do not trap iodine</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. C cells secrete calcitonin and lack the iodide-trapping mechanism of follicular cells. Only differentiated cancers — papillary and follicular — retain follicular cell function and respond to radioiodine.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>High serum calcitonin levels inactivate radioiodine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Calcitonin does not interact with radioiodine. The issue is that C cells fundamentally lack the sodium-iodide symporter needed to concentrate iodine intracellularly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The tumour capsule prevents radioiodine from reaching cancer cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Radioiodine is taken up intracellularly through iodide transporters, not by passive diffusion through a capsule. The cell of origin, not encapsulation, determines uptake.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Think cell of origin: follicular cells make thyroid hormones and trap iodine; parafollicular C cells make calcitonin and do not. This distinction separates radioiodine-responsive from radioiodine-resistant thyroid cancers."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The cell-of-origin framework determines treatment: follicular cells (papillary, follicular carcinoma) trap iodine → radioiodine works. Parafollicular C cells (medullary carcinoma) do not → radioiodine is ineffective."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
