          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Following total thyroidectomy, a patient develops carpopedal spasm and perioral </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Following total thyroidectomy, a patient develops carpopedal spasm and perioral tingling on the first postoperative day. Which complication is most likely?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RLN injury presents as hoarseness or voice change, not neuromuscular excitability. Carpopedal spasm points to a metabolic cause related to calcium homeostasis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>External laryngeal nerve injury</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ELN injury causes a weak, easily fatigued voice but does not produce spasm or perioral tingling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reactionary haemorrhage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Postoperative neck haemorrhage presents with swelling, pain, and respiratory distress from airway compression — not with carpopedal spasm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypoparathyroidism</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Accidental removal or devascularisation of the parathyroid glands causes hypocalcaemia, which manifests as carpopedal spasm, perioral tingling, and a positive Chvostek sign.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Carpopedal spasm and perioral tingling after thyroidectomy are hallmarks of hypocalcaemia from hypoparathyroidism. Always monitor serum calcium in the early postoperative period."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior surface of each thyroid lobe and depend on the inferior thyroid artery for their blood supply. Their inadvertent damage is a recognised complication, especially of total thyroidectomy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
